see ''warden''<sup>1</sup>. But the sense of ''warden'' is active, ‘one who keeps,’ and it does not seem to apply to a pear; and the ME. forms of ''warden''<sup>1</sup> are different from those of ''warden''<sup>2</sup>. Perhaps the origin is in OF. *''wardon'', a var. of ''gardon'' (Godefroy), a var. of ''gardin'', garden: see ''garden''.] A kind of pear, used chiefly for roasting or baking.
{{smaller block|''Wardone'', peere, volemum. ''Wardone'' tree, volemus.
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 516.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Faith, I would have had him roasted like a ''warden'',
In brown paper, and no more talk on ’t.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Cupid’s Revenge, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ox-cheek when hot, and ''wardens'' bak’d, some cry;
But ’tis with an intention men should buy.</poem>
{{right|''W. King'', Art of Cookery, l. 541.}}}}

'''Warden pie''', a pie made of warden pears, baked or stewed without crust.
{{smaller block|I must have saffron to colour the ''warden pies''.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 3. 48.}}}}

'''wardenry''' (wâr′dn-ri), ''n.'' [< ''warden''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ry'' (see ''-ery'').] '''1.''' The district in charge of a warden.
{{smaller block|<poem>But yet they may not tamely see,
All through the Western ''Wardenry'',
Your law-contemning kinsmen ride,
And burn and spoil the Border-side.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., iv. 24.}}}}
'''2.''' The office of warden.

'''wardenship''' (wâr′dn-ship), ''n.'' [< ''warden''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ship''.] The office of warden.
{{smaller block|His Maj. K. Cha. I. gave him the ''Wardenship'' of Merton Colledge as a reward for his service, but the times suffered him not to receive or enjoy any benefitt by it.
{{right|''Aubrey'', Lives (William Harvey).}}}}

'''warder'''<sup>1</sup> (wâr′dėr), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''wardour'',

readings.c2s1:
keep, + ''robe'', ''robbe'', garment: see ''ward''<sup>1</sup> and ''robe''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Originally, a room or large closet in which clothes were kept, and in which the making of clothes, repairing, etc., were carried on.
{{smaller block|But who that departed, Gyomar ne departed neuer, but a-bode spekynge with Morgain, the sustur of kynge Arthur, in a ''wardrope'' vnder the paleys, where she wrought with silke and golde. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 507.}}

The last day of Octobre, the . . . yere of the reyne of King Henri the Sixt, Sir John Fastolf, Knyght, hath lefte in his ''warde-drope'' at Castre this stuffe of clothys, and othir harnays that followith. {{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 475.}}

<poem>When first he spies
His Prince’s ''Wardrobe'' ope, quite through is shot
With wondring fear. ''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, iii. 75.</poem>

God clothed us; . . . he hath opened his ''wardrobe'' unto us. {{right|''Donne'', Sermons, vii.}}}}
'''2.''' A piece of furniture for the keeping of clothes, especially a large press closed by means of a door or doors, in which clothes can be hung up, and sometimes having shelves and drawers as well.
{{smaller block|There! Carter has done with you, or nearly so; I’ll make you decent in a trice. Jane, . . . open the top drawer of the ''wardrobe'', and take out a clean shirt and neck-handkerchief: bring them here; and be nimble. {{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xx.}}

A ponderous mahogany ''wardrobe'', looking like nothing so much as a grim wooden mausoleum, occupied nearly all of one wall. {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVI. 192.}}}}
'''3.''' The clothes belonging to one person at one time.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Hot.'' The king hath many marching in his coats.
''Doug.'' Now, by my sword, I will kill all his coats;
I’ll murder all his ''wardrobe'', piece by piece.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., v. 3. 27.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|The most important article of all in a gentleman’s ''wardrobe'' was still wanting. ''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 14.}}
'''4†.''' A privy.
{{smaller block|I seye that in a ''wardrobe'' they him threwe.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prioress’s Tale, l. 120.}}}}

'''wardrober''' (wârd′rō″bėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''warderopere''; < ''wardrobe'' + ''-er''<sup>2</sup>.] The keeper of a wardrobe.
{{smaller block|An indenture . . . in which Peter Curteys, the king’s ''wardrober'', undertakes to furnish by the 3rd of July the articles specified for the coronation of King Richard.
{{right|''J. Gairdner'', Richard III., iv.}}}}

'''ward-room''' (wârd′röm), ''n.'' The apartment assigned to the commissioned officers of a man-of-war other than the commanding officer. Line-officers occupy staterooms on the starboard side and staff-officers on the port side.—'''Ward-room officers''', {{smaller|commissioned officers messing in the ward-room.}}—'''Ward-room steward.''' {{smaller|See ''steward'', 2 (''b'').}}

'''wardrope†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''wardrobe''.

'''Wardrop’s disease.''' A malignant form of inflammation occurring at the root, or on one side, of a nail.

'''Wardrop’s operation for aneurism.''' See ''operation''.

'''Ward’s electuary.''' A confection of black pepper.

'''wardship<sup>1</sup>''' (wârd′ship), ''n.'' [< ''ward''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ship''.] The office of a ward or guardian; guardianship; care and protection of a ward; right of guardianship; hence, the feudal tenure by which the lord claimed the custody of the body and custody and profits of the lands of the infant heir of his deceased tenant.

